</ul>

<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<!-- 7. SYSTEM ARCHITECTURE -->
<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<h1>7. System Architecture</h1>
<p>
Project KEYSTONE implements a decoupled 3-tier architecture with an Nginx reverse proxy serving static React assets and routing API requests to the Spring Boot REST backend.
</p>

<div class="code-block">
+-------------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|                            Client Web Browser / Phone                         |
+---------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+
                                        | (HTTP / HTTPS)
                                        v
+---------------------------------------+---------------------------------------+
|                       Nginx Reverse Proxy (Port 80 / 3000)                    |
|             - Serves React 19 Single Page App static build assets             |
|             - Proxies /api/* requests to Spring Boot backend                  |
+-------------------+-----------------------------------+-----------------------+
                    |                                   |
           [Static UI Assets]                   [REST APIs & JWT]
                    v                                   v
+-------------------+-------------------+   +-----------+---------------------------+
| React 19 + Vite Frontend SPA          |   | Spring Boot 3 Backend (Port 8080)         |
| - AuthContext & Protected Routes      |   | - Stateless JWT Security Filter Chain     |
| - 7-Column Kanban Board Component     |   | - Governed State Machine Service Layer    |
| - Customer Portal & KPI Dashboard     |   | - SlaMonitoringService Background Worker  |
+---------------------------------------+   +-----------+---------------------------+
                                                        |
                                            [JPA / Hibernate / Flyway]
                                                        v
                                            +-----------+---------------------------+
                                            | PostgreSQL 16 Database (Port 5432)        |
                                            | - Relational schema (V1 to V15)           |
                                            | - Append-only audit history tables        |
                                            +-------------------------------------------+
</div>

<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<!-- 8. APPLICATION WORKFLOW -->
<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<h1>8. Application Workflow</h1>

<h3>8.1 Governed Work Order State Machine</h3>
<p>
All service tickets strictly follow a deterministic state machine. Bypassing phases (e.g. attempting to jump from <code>NEW</code> directly to <code>COMPLETED</code>) is rejected at the API layer with HTTP <code>409 Conflict</code>.
</p>

<div class="code-block">
    [*] 
     │
     ▼
   [ NEW ] ──────────(Customer / Dispatcher opens ticket)
     │
     ▼ (Dispatcher assigns technician)
 [ ASSIGNED ] 
     │
     ▼ (Technician clicks 'Start Work')
 [ IN_PROGRESS ] ◄───┐
     │         │     │ (Technician resumes work)
     │         ▼     │
     │     [ ON_HOLD ] (Waiting for parts / site access)
     │
     ▼ (Technician finishes work & logs parts/time)
 [ COMPLETED ]
     │
     ▼ (Manager performs final audit & close-out)
  [ CLOSED ] ──► [*]
</div>

<h3>8.2 End-to-End Operational Lifecycle Swimlane</h3>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Step 1 (Intake):</strong> Customer logs into Customer Portal and submits ticket (e.g. <em>"Main Server Room AC Unit Overheating"</em>). System creates <code>WorkOrder</code> with status <code>NEW</code> and computes SLA due date.</li>
  <li><strong>Step 2 (Triage & Assignment):</strong> Dispatcher reviews ticket on Kanban board, assigns available technician. Status transitions to <code>ASSIGNED</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Step 3 (Field Execution):</strong> Technician starts work (<code>IN_PROGRESS</code>), replaces parts (atomic inventory decrement), logs labor time, and marks job as <code>COMPLETED</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Step 4 (Executive Sign-Off):</strong> Manager reviews KPI analytics and signs off on the completed order, locking financials in terminal state <code>CLOSED</code>.</li>
</ol>

<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<!-- 9. DATABASE DESIGN -->
<!-- ========================================================================= -->
<h1>9. Database Design</h1>

<h3>9.1 Relational Schema Description</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong><code>users</code>:</strong> Stores credentials (email, BCrypt hashed password) and security <code>role</code> (<code>MANAGER</code>, <code>DISPATCHER</code>, <code>TECHNICIAN</code>, <code>CUSTOMER</code>).</li>
  <li><strong><code>customers</code> & <code>sites</code>:</strong> Stores client enterprise accounts and physical facility locations.</li>
  <li><strong><code>work_orders</code>:</strong> Primary entity storing tracking code (<code>WO-YYYY-0001</code>), priority, status, SLA due timestamp, and foreign keys to customer, site, and assigned technician.</li>
  <li><strong><code>work_order_status_history</code>:</strong> Append-only immutable audit trail capturing every state change, timestamp, user ID, and transition notes.</li>
  <li><strong><code>parts</code> & <code>part_usage</code>:</strong> Warehouse catalog and transactional parts consumption records.</li>
  <li><strong><code>time_logs</code>:</strong> Technician labor minutes and work notes.</li>
</ul>

